$DIR/main.p:22:3: cannot keep p in keep after this iteration: the loop at line 17 makes it in the same memory in every iteration
    A composite literal whose address is taken (at main.p:18) is made in
    literal's frame, as a local variable would be.
    Every iteration of the loop at line 17 reuses that memory. When the next
    iteration starts, keep would see that iteration's value, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Use p before the iteration ends, or keep a copy of the value instead of a
    view of it.
    Or allocate the T in each iteration with new, and keep its owner, an
    owned[T]:
        p := new(T{...})
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:33:3: cannot keep s in keep after this iteration: the loop at line 28 makes it in the same memory in every iteration
    A slice literal (at main.p:29) keeps its elements in an array in
    sliceLiteral's frame, and the slice looks at that array.
    Every iteration of the loop at line 28 reuses that memory. When the next
    iteration starts, keep would see that iteration's value, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Use s before the iteration ends, or keep a copy of the value instead of a
    view of it.
    Or keep the values in an owner such as a vec.Vec, which makes its own
    memory.
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)

